  <h2 id="system" class="scroll-mt-20">Why is it so hard to design a single screen?</h2>
  <p>That was the question my design manager asked me two weeks into this project. Privacy consent is a single moment in a flow, but it carries enormous complexity.</p>
  <p>To ship a notice that was genuinely account-level, we needed shared infrastructure that didn't yet exist: a service that could hold a user's consent record, serve the right experience based on their region and regulatory context, and propagate their choice across every Microsoft product in real time.</p>
  <p>After that question, I went back to the drawing board — and to my design process notebook — and thought: this needs a service design approach (yet again!).</p>
  <p>I started by meeting with stakeholders across engineering, legal, privacy, and product to understand how each piece of the system connected — and where the gaps were. The blueprint below is what came out of that process: a map of every layer from regulatory requirement to user-facing moment, across five stages of the consent journey.</p>

  <p class="text-sm text-gray-400">Click to expand the image.</p>
  <div class="not-prose mt-4 tablet:-mx-[10%]">
    <ImageLightbox src="/images/Privacy-Mapping-the-system.svg" alt="Mapping the system: a service blueprint across user action, UX, data request, data storage, and stakeholders, split into frontstage and backstage." />
  </div>

  <div class="not-prose grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 mt-8">
    <div>
      <h3 class="font-medium text-gray-900 mb-2">The line of visibility is the tip of the iceberg.</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Everything above it — what users see — had to feel simple and trustworthy. Everything below it had to handle extraordinary complexity: multi-region regulatory rules, real-time flag propagation, immutable audit logs for regulators. Keeping those two worlds coherent was the central tension of the project.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="font-medium text-gray-900 mb-2">Legal, engineering, and design had different definitions of success.</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Mapping the full service gave us a shared artifact that made those gaps visible before they became launch blockers. Each team could see how its decisions would impact other parts of the experience.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="research" class="scroll-mt-20">What we learned from the literature review: users need more than a thorough disclosure</h2>
  <p>A generative study and literature review surfaced three principles that shaped every design decision that followed.</p>
  <div class="not-prose grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 mt-6">
    <div>
      <h3 class="font-medium text-gray-900 mb-2">Trust is built through transparency, not reassurance.</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Users don't want to be told their data is safe — they want to see and control how it's used. Confidence comes from capability, not promises.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="font-medium text-gray-900 mb-2">Value exchange has to be obvious and worth it.</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Consent without a clear benefit felt coercive. Users needed to understand what they were getting, not just what they were giving up.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="font-medium text-gray-900 mb-2">People respond to stories, not legalese.</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Clear examples, concrete visuals, and plain language outperformed dense policy copy in every context reviewed.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="execution" class="scroll-mt-20">Execution: Designing for comprehension beyond compliance</h2>
  <p>As we partnered with legal, the text kept growing. More clauses, more caveats, more coverage. As the UX team, we pushed back on a core assumption: that saying more equals more transparency.</p>
  <p>To arrive at the best user outcome, I took several approaches to align the team. First, I ran a design workshop, which revealed a misalignment in priorities between disciplines. I used it to surface these differences in goals and anchor the discussion in shared tradeoffs. By laying out perspectives without judgment, we aligned on a balanced direction the team could collectively support and move forward with.</p>

  <div class="not-prose mt-6 tablet:-mx-[10%]">
    <ImageLightbox src="/images/Privacy-Workshop-board.png" alt="Sign-in notice design feedback workshop board: three design variants voted on for best comprehension, best UX, and best consent rate" />
  </div>

  <p>Second, I brought in the user's voice as evidence of how we could succeed on both compliance and product goals. I designed a privacy comprehension study embedded in a realistic task flow: users were asked to sign in to edit a document, then immediately asked what they remembered seeing. By using comprehension as the benchmark, the design could meet both user needs and privacy disclosure requirements. We tested three layouts: a block of text, an accordion, and a paragraph format with bolded headers.</p>

  <div class="not-prose mt-6 tablet:-mx-[10%]">
    <ImageLightbox src="/images/Privacy-User-test-Comprehension.png" alt="Three consent dialog layouts tested side by side: Brief 1 paragraph, Accordion, and Paragraphs with bolded headers — all in the Microsoft SISU dialog" />
  </div>

  <p>What we found reshaped the design in three ways.</p>
  <div class="not-prose grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 mt-6">
    <div>
      <h3 class="font-medium text-gray-900 mb-2">Layout: paragraphs win, but length is still a risk.</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">The paragraph format — bolded header, descriptive text below — outperformed both alternatives on recall. But users flagged the tension themselves: "Would I actually read this much when I'm just trying to open Word?" Comprehension and patience aren't the same thing.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="font-medium text-gray-900 mb-2">Button strings carry more weight than we assumed.</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">Users were reading CTAs as signals about where they were in the process. "Sign in" made users feel they hadn't fully authenticated yet — creating confusion when paired with a consent notice. We tested "Sign in," "Continue," and "Got it" and found the string could either anchor or undermine the experience.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="font-medium text-gray-900 mb-2">Word choice at the micro level changed perceived risk.</h3>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">"Sync" read as exposure. "Save" read as security. Two words, same underlying action, completely different trust response. Every word in the consent notice was load-bearing.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>Next, we checked our design against the learnings from the literature review and brought everything together as a visual narrative. I partnered with the brand design team to find a metaphor we could use.</p>

  <div class="not-prose grid grid-cols-3 gap-4 tablet:gap-8 mt-6">
    {[
      { src: '/images/Security.svg', label: 'Security' },
      { src: '/images/Sync.svg', label: 'Sync' },
      { src: '/images/Control.svg', label: 'Control' },
    ].map(m => (
      <figure>
        <img src={m.src} alt={`${m.label} illustration metaphor`} class="w-full h-auto rounded-lg border border-gray-100" />
        <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm font-medium text-gray-700">{m.label}</figcaption>
      </figure>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>Understanding the legal team's concern that over-indexing on privacy or security would put an unnecessary burden on compliance, we chose the 'sync' visual metaphor to communicate the unified experience that consent unlocks.</p>
  <p>The outcome: we prioritized user comprehension as the shared metric — it was the one value every stakeholder, from legal to engineering to privacy advocates, could rally behind.</p>

  <h2 id="impact" class="scroll-mt-20">Impact</h2>
  <!-- Final design floats left; impact text and quotes wrap beside and below it -->
